      "annotatedScript": "[STILLNESS] On the morning of August 14, 2018, Tomas Reyes stood on the gravel apron outside the Bitterroot Ranger Station in western Montana, squinting at a sky the color of tarnished pewter. [PAUSE 1.5s] Tomas was thirty-four, six years into running a twenty-person hotshot crew, and his mornings always began the same way: black coffee in a dented steel thermos, a grease-penciled topographic map spread across the hood of a white crew buggy, and the smell of pine pitch baking off the floorboards. [BEAT] His crew called him Steady, half in respect and half in teasing, because Tomas never raised his voice on a fireline. He believed noise was just panic wearing boots. [SCENE SHIFT] By noon, they were four miles up Timber Creek Ridge, cutting a four-foot scratch line through cheatgrass and lodgepole pine. The fire\u2014the Rattlesnake Complex\u2014was supposed to be three ridges over, chewing through old timber at a predictable two chains an hour. [LOWER VOICE] That was what the morning briefing had promised. [PAUSE 1.5s] Tomas knelt beside a charred stump, scraping his Pulaski into the duff, when he noticed something small and wrong. A column of gray ash was drifting downhill toward them, not uphill. [EMPHASIS: the wind was lying] The weather dispatch had called for steady westerlies at eight miles an hour. Yet the smoke in the draw below was curling back on itself like a fist. [BEAT] Tomas stood up, keyed his shoulder mic, and called his lookout two miles south. \"Hollis, give me a read on the lower saddle.\" Static hissed back. Then Hollis's voice, thin and clipped: \"Still holding west, Boss. You're good.\" Tomas looked at the ash settling on his Nomex sleeve. His crew was sweating through their yellow shirts, twenty yards apart, swinging tools in rhythm. Behind them, the ridge dropped into a narrow, brush-choked chute with only one way out: a steep, rocky two-track that took twelve minutes to hike at a dead run. [SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] Tomas walked fifty yards up the line to his sawyer lead, Mara Vance, who was fueling a chainsaw with her visor flipped up. \"Mara, pull the saws. Tell the tail to stage packs at the turn.\" Mara blinked through a smear of bar oil. \"Boss? We've got forty minutes of daylight left on this section. Division Chief wants this tied into the creek before dark.\" [LEAN IN] \"I know what Division wants,\" Tomas said, keeping his voice flat. \"The smoke in the draw is reversing.\" Mara glanced over the lip of the ridge. From where they stood, the main plume still looked miles away, a clean white pillar against the haze. \"That's just terrain eddy, Tomas. Happens every afternoon at two.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Behind her, two younger crew members\u2014kids in their first season\u2014stopped swinging and looked over. Tomas felt the weight of nineteen pairs of eyes. If he pulled them off the line now, Division Chief Halverson would blister him on the evening tactical net. Worse, his crew would think he had lost his nerve. [EMPHASIS: nobody writes a thank-you note for a false alarm] \"Hold five minutes,\" Mara said softly, stepping closer so the rookies wouldn't hear. \"Let's not be the crew that bails on a clean line.\" Tomas looked at the map tucked into his chest pocket, then back at the draw. He hated the feeling in his stomach\u2014not fear, exactly, but the sudden, sickening suspicion that he was about to look foolish. \"Five minutes,\" he agreed. \"Keep packs on.\" [SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] Those five minutes stretched like wet leather. Tomas walked back to the edge of the chute alone. The air had gone strangely cool, the way it does right before a summer hailstorm. No birds were singing in the ponderosas. [LOWER VOICE] He pulled out his pocket weather meter, held it up, and watched the little impeller spin\u2014three miles an hour, then zero, then five from the east. [PAUSE 2s] The dispatch wasn't just wrong; it was twelve hours stale, built on a ridge-top sensor that had been knocked offline by lightning the night before. Tomas hadn't known that. Nobody on the ridge knew that. [BEAT] He stood there with the plastic meter in his calloused palm, feeling every ounce of his six years as a boss press down on his shoulders. If he stayed, and the wind was just a local quirk, he kept his reputation. If he pulled the plug, he looked timid in front of Division, in front of Mara, in front of nineteen people who trusted him to read the mountain. [EMPHASIS: ten seconds of pride costs nineteen lives] His thumb hovered over the radio button. He thought about his own father, a retired millwright who used to say that the most dangerous man in any shop was the one who couldn't afford to look wrong. Tomas's throat was dry. The ash was thicker now, stinging his eyes. [SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] He didn't wait for the fifth minute. He keyed the mic, his voice low and unmistakable. \"All hands, Bitterroot Four. Drop tools. Leave the saws. Move to the two-track now.\" [SPEED UP] Mara didn't argue this time. She saw the way Tomas was already walking backward, eyes locked on the timber below. \"Move!\" she barked, and the crew scrambled, boots slipping on loose scree. [BEAT] They were four minutes up the two-track, lungs burning, when the sound hit them\u2014not a crackle, but a low, freight-train roar that vibrated through the soles of their boots. Tomas turned just once. Where they had been standing six minutes earlier, a wall of orange flame forty feet high was crowning through the lodgepoles, eating the scratch line whole. [PAUSE 2s] Nobody spoke for the rest of the hike out. They reached the safety zone at the gravel turnout with soot in their teeth and their packs half-unbuckled. Half an hour later, Division Chief Halverson's pickup rolled up in a cloud of dust. Halverson stepped out, looked at the blackened ridge above them, then looked at Tomas. \"Good call, Reyes,\" Halverson said, and walked back to his truck. [SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] That night, Tomas couldn't sleep. He sat at a picnic table outside the spike camp under a sodium floodlight, staring at his grease-penciled map. [LOWER VOICE] Everyone at camp had congratulated him. They called it instinct. They called it leadership. [PAUSE 1.5s] Tomas knew better. It hadn't been instinct. He had almost waited five minutes just to save face. He had almost let a stale weather report and a fear of looking weak decide whether nineteen people came down that mountain. [EMPHASIS: the map had a blind spot] The next morning, instead of going straight to the morning briefing, Tomas sat in the cab of his buggy with a yellow legal pad and started writing down every single gap between what dispatch saw and what the crew on the ground actually felt. [WARM SMILE] He didn't set out to build a company. He didn't even know the word for what he was sketching\u2014a simple, rugged mesh of pocket sensors that crews could clip to their packs so nobody ever had to trust a broken ridge-top tower again. [BEAT] Three years later, when investors asked Tomas how he came up with the idea for his wildfire-telemetry startup, he never told them he was a visionary. [STILLNESS] He told them about a five-minute argument on a dry ridge, a plastic wind meter in a shaking hand, and the realization that [EMPHASIS: the work was never about being right]\u2014it was about having the nerve to listen when the mountain whispers that you're wrong. [PAUSE 2s]",
