      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] Three days before the November opening at the Clemens-Hartman Museum in Providence, the rain came down in cold, sideways sheets against the basement clerestory windows. [SLOW DOWN] Inside Conservation Lab B, the air smelled of linseed oil, damp wool, and old paper. [BEAT] Dr. Nadia Okafor had been the curator of architectural drawings for eleven years, and she liked the quiet of the basement. Upstairs, the galleries were full of ladders, drywall dust, and nervous trustees. Downstairs, there was only the hum of the dehumidifier and the long oak table under her halogen lamp.\n\nShe was doing the final condition check on Lot Fourteen\u2014a 1924 watercolor of the Narragansett Pier by Arthur Pendelton. [LEAN IN] It was not just another drawing. It was the centerpiece of the entire winter exhibition, loaned by Harrison Vance, the board chair whose family foundation had underwritten half the museum's new wing. The catalog was already printed. The banners were already hanging on the limestone facade outside.\n\nNadia adjusted the raking light so it grazed the paper at a fifteen-degree angle. [LOWER VOICE] That was her habit\u2014never look straight down at a surface when you can look across it. Under direct light, the watercolor looked flawless: pale cobalt sky, crisp ink pilings, and in the lower-right corner, Pendelton's familiar, looping signature in iron-gall brown. [PAUSE 1.5s] But under the raking beam, something caught. A tiny ridge. A gloss where there should have been matte tooth.\n\nShe picked up her ten-power loupe and leaned in until her forehead nearly touched the glass. [EMPHASIS: the ink was sitting on top of the varnish]. In 1952, a restorer had brushed a thin coat of dammar resin over the sheet to stabilize the pigment. Every stroke of Pendelton's brush was sealed beneath that layer. [BEAT] Every stroke except the signature. The signature had been added decades after the artist died, inked right over the 1952 seal.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] Nadia did not call the press. She did not call the police. She took three macro photographs, printed them on matte paper, and walked upstairs to the director's office at four in the afternoon. Martin Reed was standing behind his desk, still in his raincoat, reviewing the catering seating chart.\n\nShe laid the prints beside the seating chart. \"The Pendelton is a ghost,\" she said. \"The drawing is period. The signature is modern.\"\n\nMartin did not pick up the loupe she offered. He looked at the prints for four seconds, then slid them back across the mahogany. \"Harrison Vance has forty guests flying in from Boston on Thursday,\" he said. \"His mother's name is on the endowment plaque.\"\n\n\"If we hang it as an authentic Pendelton,\" Nadia said, \"we are certifying a forgery.\"\n\nMartin sighed, the patient sigh of a man who managed budgets rather than paper. \"Nadia, it's a watercolor of a pier. [EMPHASIS: Nobody looks at the corner]. Leave the label as 'Attributed to' in the second printing if it bothers you. Right now, we hang the centerpiece.\"\n\n\"Attributed to\" was museum shorthand for a polite lie. She stood in the doorway for a moment, watching him go back to his seating chart. [PAUSE 1.5s] She realized then that Martin wasn't evil. He was just tired, and he wanted the opening to go smoothly.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] By eleven that night, the museum was empty except for the night watchman on the first floor. Nadia sat back down at the oak table in Lab B. Her shoulders ached. Her eyes burned from the halogen lamp. [BEAT] On the left side of her desk sat the Pendelton watercolor. On the right sat the draft of her resignation letter, which she had started writing at nine and deleted twice.\n\nIf she pulled the piece without Martin's sign-off, she would be fired before the wine was poured on Thursday. If she hung it, she would keep her pension, her title, and her quiet basement. [LOWER VOICE] Nobody in the public would ever know. Not one trustee would bring a fifteen-degree raking light to a cocktail party.\n\nShe picked up the phone and called the one person who might tell her she was crazy: Arthur's great-nephew, a retired archivist living in Mystic. He didn't answer. She left no message. [STILLNESS] In the silence after the dial tone died, she looked at the watercolor again. She wasn't angry at Harrison Vance, who had probably bought the piece in good faith from a crooked dealer in the seventies. She wasn't even angry at Martin. She was angry at how easy it was to let it slide. [PAUSE 2s] That was the trap\u2014not malice, just the gravitational pull of not making a scene.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At six-thirty the next morning, Nadia walked into the main gallery before the drywall crew arrived. She did not bring the watercolor. She brought a brass placard and a wooden easel. [LEAN IN] When Harrison Vance arrived at ten for his private preview, Martin was waiting by the entrance, smiling, ready to steer him toward the east wall. Instead, Vance stopped in front of the centerpiece space.\n\nThere was no watercolor on the wall. There was [EMPHASIS: an empty frame with a brass placard] beneath it that read: *Study of Narragansett Pier, c. 1924. Removed from exhibition pending provenance verification.*\n\nMartin's face went the color of wet chalk. \"Nadia,\" he whispered, \"what have you done?\"\n\nVance didn't look at Martin. He leaned toward the empty frame, squinting at the placard, then turned to Nadia. \"My father bought that in seventy-four,\" he said, his voice tight. \"Are you telling me he was cheated?\"\n\n\"I'm telling you,\" Nadia said steadily, \"that the paper is real, the sky is real, and the signature was added after nineteen-fifty-two. If we hang it, we lie to your family and to everyone who walks through that door.\"\n\n[PAUSE 1.5s] The gallery went dead quiet. A drill hummed two rooms away and then stopped. Vance looked at the empty frame for a long time. \"It's going to look like a hole in the wall,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes,\" Nadia said. \"An honest hole.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [WARM SMILE] Vance did not pull his funding. He stood there for another minute, nodded once, and asked her to show him the macro photographs downstairs. [BEAT] The exhibition opened on Thursday night with an empty frame on the east wall. More people stopped in front of that empty frame than any other piece in the building.\n\n[SLOW DOWN] Nadia never gave a speech about integrity. She didn't start a movement on stage. She simply wrote a three-page protocol for raking-light and varnish-layer testing that every incoming loan at the Clemens-Hartman still has to pass. [EMPHASIS: convenience is just a polite word for complicity]. Three years later, when she founded an independent authentication lab for regional museums, people asked her when she knew she wanted to build it. [PAUSE 2s] She never said she had a vision. She just told them about a Tuesday in November, a fifteen-degree lamp, and an empty frame that refused to lie.",
