      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] In November of 2016, Nadia Okafor worked out of a borrowed basement office on East Broad Street in Columbus, Ohio, where the radiator clanked like a dropped wrench every twenty minutes. [PAUSE 1.5s] She was thirty-one years old, a forensic accountant who specialized in municipal grants and small-scale compliance, and she liked her work precisely because it had no glamour. Numbers did not flatter anyone. Numbers did not ask for applause. Every morning at seven-thirty, Nadia hung her damp trench coat on a brass hook behind the door, poured black coffee into a chipped ceramic mug, and opened whatever shoebox of receipts a client had been too embarrassed to organize. [BEAT] That Tuesday, the shoebox belonged to the Hearthstone Youth Literacy Trust, a beloved neighborhood charity that funded after-school reading tutors across Franklin County. On paper, Hearthstone was a triumph. Its annual gala raised four hundred thousand dollars. Its executive director, a former high school principal named Arthur Vance, was quoted in the local paper every spring. [LEAN IN] Yet the board's new treasurer had noticed something small\u2014not a missing million, not a headline scandal, just a recurring shortfall in the summer tutoring budget that never quite matched the donor pledges. [PAUSE 1.5s] Nadia was hired for forty hours at a modest hourly rate to reconcile three years of bank statements and put the board's mind at ease.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By Thursday afternoon, Nadia's desk was buried under accordion folders, printed general ledgers, and yellow legal pads covered in her tight, vertical handwriting. [SPEED UP] At first, everything looked spotless. Every check had two signatures. Every grant disbursement matched a signed invoice. Every catering bill from the annual gala had a corresponding receipt. Nadia ran a Benford's Law frequency test on the vendor payments, expecting the usual sloppy bookkeeping of an understaffed nonprofit. Instead, the distribution was almost unnaturally clean. [SLOW DOWN] Too clean. [PAUSE 1.5s] She pulled out Folder Nine\u2014miscellaneous printing and curriculum supplies\u2014and noticed a pattern that made her set her pen down. Seventeen different payments over fourteen months, each between two hundred and eighty and three hundred and forty dollars, all payable to a company called Buckeye Meridian Press. [EMPHASIS: three hundred and twelve dollars at a time.] Not a single invoice exceeded the three-hundred-and-fifty-dollar threshold that required secondary board approval. Nadia called Arthur Vance's office to ask for the physical delivery slips. Twenty minutes later, Vance himself walked down the basement stairs, carrying two coffees he hadn't been asked to bring. [WARM SMILE] \"Nadia, you're working too hard,\" he said, setting a cup beside her elbow. \"Buckeye Meridian is just our local print shop for phonics workbooks. Old man who runs it still uses carbon-copy pads. If you're hunting for a villain down here, you're going to be disappointed.\" When Nadia asked for the old man's phone number, Vance's smile didn't flicker, but his hand stayed on the doorknob. \"He retired in August,\" Vance said softly. \"Closed up shop. Honestly, Nadia, the board's just nervous because we're expanding. Don't burn forty hours chasing ghosts.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] That night, the rain turned to sleet against the high basement windows. At eleven-fifteen, Nadia was still at her desk, her eyes burning from the fluorescent tube overhead. Her neck ached. Her phone buzzed with a text from the board treasurer asking if she was ready to wrap up by Friday so they could announce a clean audit at Monday's donor breakfast. [PAUSE 1.5s] For a long moment, Nadia stared at the blank summary page on her screen. She could write the standard letter. She could note \"minor documentation gaps due to vendor closure,\" collect her fee, and go home to sleep. Nobody would blame her. In fact, everyone in that building would thank her. [BEAT] She felt the quiet, suffocating weight of being the only person in the room who wanted to keep looking. What if she was wrong? What if she accused a respected educator of theft over a few missing carbon copies and ruined her own reputation in a city where everyone knew everyone? [STILLNESS] She stood up, walked to the sink, and splashed cold water on her face. Then she sat back down, opened the Ohio Secretary of State's business registry on her laptop, and typed in Buckeye Meridian Press. There was no print shop. There had never been a print shop. The registered agent was a post office box in Westerville, opened three weeks before the first three-hundred-and-twelve-dollar check cleared. [PAUSE 2s] Nadia didn't feel triumphant. She felt cold.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] At eight o'clock the next morning, Nadia didn't call the police, and she didn't call the press. She walked upstairs to the second-floor conference room where Arthur Vance was reviewing seating charts for the donor breakfast. She carried only Folder Nine and a single sheet of paper. Vance looked up, irritated. \"I thought we agreed you were wrapping up,\" he said. Nadia placed the paper on the mahogany table between them. [SLOW DOWN] \"You told me Buckeye Meridian was a retired printer,\" she said, keeping her voice level. \"The post office box belongs to a shell LLC registered to your brother-in-law's address. Over thirty-eight months, you wrote one hundred and forty-two checks just under the board's approval limit. Forty-four thousand dollars.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Vance didn't shout. He didn't slam his fist. He leaned back, folded his hands, and looked at her the way a tired principal looks at a student who doesn't understand how the world works. \"Forty-four thousand dollars,\" he repeated, almost gently. \"Nadia, I raised four million for these kids. I worked seventy hours a week for twelve years. My wife's medical bills wiped us out in 2013, and I took a fraction of what a corporate executive makes. If you blow this up, the donors will pull out, and three hundred kids lose their tutors. Is your little spreadsheet worth that?\" [PAUSE 2s] The room was completely silent. Nadia looked at the seating chart, then back at Vance. [EMPHASIS: nobody steals from a reading room in round numbers.] \"You didn't take it from the donors, Arthur,\" she said quietly. \"You took it from the summer tutoring budget. That's why the kids in the south-end schools didn't have books in July. [EMPHASIS: the math didn't care about his charm.] And neither do I.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Nadia delivered her report to the board at noon. Vance resigned before five o'clock, and the charity's insurance covered the loss within sixty days, keeping every tutoring classroom open through the following summer. [PAUSE 1.5s] Nadia didn't hold a press conference. She didn't put the story on a billboard. She simply packed her legal pads back into her briefcase, walked up the basement stairs into the cold November air, and realized something had permanently shifted in her chest. [SLOW DOWN] Before that week, she had thought of accounting as a way to keep score after the game was over. Now she understood it was the only thing standing between a good intention and a quiet betrayal. [BEAT] Six months later, she started her own firm\u2014not to chase headlines, and not to preach about integrity, but to build automated ledger checks for small nonprofits so no board would ever have to rely on luck or charm again. [WARM SMILE] When people ask her how her company began, she never talks about vision or disruption. She just points to a framed photocopy of Folder Nine on her wall\u2014[EMPHASIS: not a crusade\u2014just a refusal to look away.] [PAUSE 2s]",
